5what if i dont know typescript do i need to learn it ‡**No, you do not need to learn TypeScript right away.** You can start Playwright using standard JavaScript today because Playwright natively supports both languages out of the box.

If learning TypeScript feels like an extra barrier right now, skip it for your first few weeks and stick to JavaScript. However, knowing where TypeScript fits into your long-term roadmap will help you plan your learning effectively.

**JavaScript vs. TypeScript for Playwright**

| Feature | JavaScript (Start Here) | TypeScript (Target Later) |
|---|---|---|
| **Learning Curve** | Zero extra setup if you already know basic JS. | Requires learning basic typing syntax (`string`, `number`, interfaces). |
| **Playwright Compatibility** | 100% supported natively. | 100% supported natively (built-in compiler). |
| **Code Auto-complete** | Basic IntelliSense in VS Code. | Superior IntelliSense; suggests Playwright methods instantly. |
| **Job Market Demand** | Common for junior QA/Automation roles. | Preferred for Mid/Senior SDET positions in modern tech stacks. |

**The Strategy for Your Shift**

* **Phase 1 (Now): Write tests in JavaScript.** Focus on understanding Playwright locators, assertions, and test structure without worrying about type errors.
* **Phase 2 (Next Month): Transition to TypeScript.** TypeScript is literally just JavaScript with type annotations attached to variables and functions. Once you understand Playwright syntax in JS, adding TypeScript takes only a day or two of learning.

Start your Playwright setup using JavaScript so you can build momentum immediately instead of getting stuck studying another language first.

