1you better help me setup playwright in my system ØLet's get Playwright installed and running on your machine right now. 

Before we type any commands, ensure you have **Node.js** installed on your system (you can check by opening your terminal and typing `node -v`). You will also need a code editor like **VS Code**.

Here is the exact sequence to set up a new Playwright project using JavaScript:

<Sequence>
  <Step title="Create a new project folder">
    Open your terminal, create a new folder for your automation project, and navigate into it:
    ```bash
    mkdir my-playwright-project
    cd my-playwright-project
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Run the installation command">
    Run the official Playwright initializer. This command downloads the framework and sets up the required folders.
    ```bash
    npm init playwright@latest
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step subtitle="Crucial: Select JavaScript here" title="Answer the setup prompts">
    The terminal will ask you a series of questions. Answer them exactly like this:
    * **Do you want to use TypeScript or JavaScript?** -> Select **JavaScript** (use arrow keys)
    * **Where to put your end-to-end tests?** -> Press Enter to accept the default (`tests`)
    * **Add a GitHub Actions workflow?** -> Type `y` or `n` (you can choose `n` for now to keep it simple)
    * **Install Playwright browsers?** -> Press Enter to accept (`true`). *This might take a minute as it downloads Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit.*
  </Step>

  <Step title="Run the default tests">
    Playwright automatically created a sample test file for you. Let's run it to prove everything is working.
    ```bash
    npx playwright test
    ```
    You will see the tests execute in the background (headless mode) in your terminal.
  </Step>

  <Step title="View the HTML report">
    Once the tests finish, Playwright generates a detailed visual report. Open it with:
    ```bash
    npx playwright show-report
    ```
    This will open a browser tab showing exactly what passed, how long it took, and what steps were executed.
  </Step>
</Sequence>

Once you complete these steps, open the `my-playwright-project` folder in VS Code. Look inside the `tests` folderâ€”you will see a file called `example.spec.js`. That is your very first test script.

<FollowUp label="Want to write a custom test together?" query="Help me write my first custom Playwright test to open a website and click a button."/>½
;the first two dependencies are missing how to install them ı
When Playwright mentions "missing dependencies," it usually means your operating system is missing some background libraries required to run the browsers, or the Node packages didn't download completely. 

Here are the commands to fix both scenarios directly in your terminal.

**1. Install System/Browser Dependencies**
If Playwright failed while setting up Chromium, Firefox, or WebKit (very common on Linux or Windows WSL), it has a built-in command to automatically fetch what is missing. Run these two commands one after the other:

```bash
npx playwright install-deps
```
Once that finishes, tell Playwright to try downloading the browsers again:
```bash
npx playwright install
```

**2. Install Node Packages**
If the error is about missing project files (like `@playwright/test` or `playwright`), force your Node package manager to download everything listed in your project again:

```bash
npm install
```

**3. "Command not found" Error**
If your terminal actually said `npm is not recognized` or `npm: command not found`, that means Node.js is not installed on your system. You will need to download and install the "LTS" (Long Term Support) version from [nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org/), restart your terminal, and start the setup process from the beginning.

<FollowUp label="Did one of these fix the issue?" query="Yes, that fixed it. What is the next step to write my first custom test?"/>µ
Sis it not installing because i dont have chrome and firefox could it be the reason İ
No, not having Chrome or Firefox installed on your computer is not the reason it is failing. 

Playwright does not use your computer's regular web browsers. Instead, it downloads its own isolated versions of Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit and stores them in a hidden cache folder. 

When you see a "missing dependencies" error, it means your **operating system** is missing some background software libraries (like specific C++ redistributables on Windows or media libraries on Linux) that these isolated browsers need to launch. 

To fix this, you need to run the command that forces Playwright to install both the browsers and their required OS-level libraries at the same time.

Run this command in your terminal:

```bash
npx playwright install --with-deps
```

This single command will:
1. Download any missing background OS libraries.
2. Download the Playwright browser binaries again to ensure nothing was corrupted.

Once that command finishes, verify the installation by running the default test again:

```bash
npx playwright test
```

