again the same error ÀIf you are seeing **Exit Code 100** again, it means your Linux system's package manager (`apt`) is in a broken state or failing to connect to the internet to download the background libraries. 

Playwright is just the messenger hereâ€”your operating system is rejecting the software installation. Let's fix your system's package manager directly.

Run these commands one by one to repair it:

<Sequence>
  <Step title="Fix broken background packages">
    This forces Linux to repair any partially installed software that might be blocking new installations.
    ```bash
    sudo apt --fix-broken install
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Force a system package update">
    Refresh your system's download links so it knows exactly where to get the files.
    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Retry the dependency install with detailed logging">
    We will run the Playwright command again, but this time we add `DEBUG=pw:install` so if it fails, it will tell us the exact file it choked on.
    ```bash
    sudo DEBUG=pw:install npx playwright install-deps
    ```
  </Step>
</Sequence>

If the third command completes successfully, you are in the clear and can run `npx playwright install` to finish up. 

<ElicitationsGroup message="If the third command failed again, look closely at the red error text it printed:">
  <Elicitation label="It mentions 'Connection timed out' or '404'" query="The error text mentions network issues like 'Connection timed out', '404 Not Found', or 'Failed to fetch'."/>
  <Elicitation label="It mentions 'Could not get lock'" query="The error text says 'Could not get lock /var/lib/dpkg/lock' or something about another process using it."/>
  <Elicitation label="It finally worked!" query="That fixed the system packages and it installed successfully. What's next?"/>
</ElicitationsGroup>—
