{N: See apt-secure(8) manpage for repository creation and user configuration details.
Failed to install browser dependenciesœThat `apt-secure` message tells us the exact problem: your Linux software manager (`apt`) is blocking system updates because one of your software repositories has an invalid, changed, or missing security signature. 

Because Playwright's installer runs `apt-get update` behind the scenes to fetch dependencies, your operating system aborts the entire process before Playwright can download anything.

Here is how to resolve the security lock and get `apt` updating cleanly:

<Sequence>
  <Step title="Force apt to accept repository changes">
    Run this command to tell your system to allow repository release changes:
    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update --allow-releaseinfo-change
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Verify apt updates cleanly">
    Run a standard update to make sure there are no other blocking repository errors:
    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Retry the Playwright dependency installation">
    Now that the system package manager is unblocked, run the Playwright setup command again:
    ```bash
    sudo npx playwright install-deps
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Install Playwright browsers and test">
    Finish up by installing the browser binaries and running the sample test:
    ```bash
    npx playwright install
    npx playwright test
    ```
  </Step>
</Sequence>

<ElicitationsGroup message="What happened after running step 1 and 2?">
  <Elicitation label="It worked, tests passed!" query="It worked! The dependencies installed and the test passed."/>
  <Elicitation label="apt-get update showed an error" query="When I ran 'sudo apt-get update', it printed a specific error about a repository."/>
</ElicitationsGroup>à
no dont force help me fix it ¾Fixing the repository properly is much safer than bypassing security checks. 

This error happens when `apt` checks the security signatures of all your software sources. If a single third-party source (like an old PPA or a custom tool repository) has an expired key or broken signature, `apt` stops all system updates to protect your machine.

Here is how to locate the broken source and clean it up cleanly:

<Steps>
  <Step title="Run apt-get update to find the broken repo">
    Execute this command to print your full repository list:
    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update
    ```
    Look at the output. Near the bottom, right above `N: See apt-secure(8)...`, you will see a line starting with **`E:` (Error)** or **`W:` (Warning)** naming a specific URL or PPA (e.g., `[http://ppa.launchpad.net/](http://ppa.launchpad.net/)...` or `[http://packages.domain.com/](http://packages.domain.com/)...`).
  </Step>

  <Step title="Inspect your third-party repository list">
    Third-party software repositories live in a dedicated folder. List all of them by running:
    ```bash
    ls /etc/apt/sources.list.d/
    ```
    You will see a list of `.list` files matching the third-party software installed on your machine.
  </Step>

  <Step title="Remove the broken repository file">
    Once you match the broken URL from **Step 1** to a file in **Step 2**, delete that specific broken repository configuration file:
    ```bash
    sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/FILENAME.list
    ```
    *(Replace `FILENAME.list` with the actual file name printed in step 2).*
  </Step>

  <Step title="Verify apt is healthy again">
    Run the update again to confirm there are zero errors:
    ```bash
    sudo apt-get update
    ```
    If it finishes cleanly without any `E:` or `W:` lines, your system's package manager is officially repaired.
  </Step>

  <Step title="Complete Playwright setup">
    Now run the standard Playwright dependency command:
    ```bash
    sudo npx playwright install-deps
    ```
  </Step>
</Steps>

<Elicitations message="What did `sudo apt-get update` output in Step 1?">
  <Elicitation label="Printed an E: error" query="I ran sudo apt-get update and got an E: error. Here is what it says."/>
  <Elicitation label="It updated cleanly!" query="Removing the broken repo worked! sudo apt-get update completed with zero errors."/>
</Elicitations>
-the first two test failed while running test ÜThe fact that the tests ran means your Playwright installation and browser dependencies are officially fixed. 

When the default sample tests fail on a fresh setup, it is almost always due to **network timeouts** (the test tries to navigate to `[https://playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev)`) or **resource limits** from running multiple browser instances at once in Linux/WSL.

Here is how to check the exact error and run the test reliably:

<Steps>
  <Step title="Open the test report to see the error">
    Playwright captures screenshots and failure logs automatically. Open the report by running:
    ```bash
    npx playwright show-report
    ```
    Click on one of the failed tests. Look at the red error lineâ€”it will usually say `Timeout 30000ms exceeded` while navigating to `[https://playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev)`.
  </Step>

  <Step title="Run tests with a single worker">
    By default, Playwright tries to launch multiple browser windows in parallel, which can overload a virtual machine or WSL environment. Force it to run one test at a time:
    ```bash
    npx playwright test --workers=1
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Run in headed mode to see what happens">
    If you want to watch the browser open and perform the test actions visually, run:
    ```bash
    npx playwright test --headed
    ```
  </Step>
</Steps>

<Elicitations message="What does the report say, or did running with --workers=1 succeed?">
  <Elicitation label="Single worker worked!" query="Running with --workers=1 passed! Let's write a custom test script now."/>
  <Elicitation label="It says Timeout exceeded" query="The report says 'Timeout 30000ms exceeded' while opening [https://playwright.dev](https://playwright.dev)."/>
</Elicitations>Š
+well the webkit for 1 and 2 not installing ÚIn Playwright's default setup, every test runs against three browser engines: **Chromium**, **Firefox**, and **WebKit**. On Linux and WSL, WebKit (Apple's Safari engine) often fails because it depends on specific Linux media and rendering libraries that aren't natively supported in virtualized environments.

Since you are learning Playwright, **you do not need WebKit running right now.** Industry web testing relies heavily on Chromium (Chrome/Edge).

Here are the three ways to handle this:

---

### Option 1: Run your tests using Chromium only (Recommended)
You can target Chromium directly in your terminal command. This bypasses WebKit entirely:

```bash
npx playwright test --project=chromium
```

---

### Option 2: Disable WebKit in your configuration
To stop Playwright from attempting to run WebKit altogether:

1. Open `playwright.config.js` in VS Code.
2. Look for the `projects:` array.
3. Find the WebKit section and comment it out or delete it:

```javascript
projects: [
  {
    name: 'chromium',
    use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'] },
  },
  {
    name: 'firefox',
    use: { ...devices['Desktop Firefox'] },
  },
  /* Comment out WebKit while learning on Linux/WSL
  {
    name: 'webkit',
    use: { ...devices['Desktop Safari'] },
  },
  */
],
```

---

### Option 3: Force WebKit installation
If you strictly want WebKit working on your machine, force Playwright to download its binary and dependencies individually:

```bash
npx playwright install webkit
```
```bash
sudo npx playwright install-deps webkit
```

