Now let's write your very first custom test script from scratch.

<Steps>
  <Step title="Create a new test file">
    Inside your `tests` folder, create a new file named `first-test.spec.js`. You can do this inside VS Code or by running this command in your terminal:
    ```bash
    touch tests/first-test.spec.js
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Paste this basic test code">
    Open `tests/first-test.spec.js` in VS Code and paste the following code:

    ```javascript
    const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

    test('Verify example domain heading and title', async ({ page }) => {
      // 1. Navigate to the website
      await page.goto('https://example.com');

      // 2. Assert the browser window title contains "Example Domain"
      await expect(page).toHaveTitle(/Example Domain/);

      // 3. Find the main heading element using a CSS locator
      const heading = page.locator('h1');

      // 4. Assert the heading is visible on screen and contains expected text
      await expect(heading).toBeVisible();
      await expect(heading).toHaveText('Example Domain');
    });
    ```
  </Step>

  <Step title="Execute your test in Chromium">
    Run only your new test file against the Chromium browser:
    ```bash
    npx playwright test tests/first-test.spec.js --project=chromium
    ```
  </Step>
</Steps>

When execution finishes, Playwright will output `1 passed` in green text in your terminal.

