FCodegen opened! I see it writing code automatically as I click around.õThat magic moment is how real SDETs save hours of manual setup. 

Codegen handles the repetitive work of finding element names and writing basic actions. However, Codegen only records **actions** (clicks and typing)â€”it doesn't know what you want to **verify**. An SDET's job is to take generated code and add assertions.

Here is the 3-step workflow to turn recorded code into a production-ready test:

**1. Copy the Recorded Code**
Inside the Playwright Inspector window, click the **Copy** button (top right icon) to save the generated code to your clipboard. Close the Inspector window when you're done.

**2. Paste and Add Assertions**
Open `tests/first-test.spec.js` in VS Code, replace the content with your copied code, and add `expect()` statements at the end to verify your results:

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('Recorded workflow with assertions', async ({ page }) => {
  // --- Paste Codegen steps below ---
  await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/#/');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('What needs to be done?').fill('Buy groceries');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('What needs to be done?').press('Enter');

  // --- Add SDET Assertions manually below ---
  const item = page.getByText('Buy groceries');
  await expect(item).toBeVisible();
});
```

**3. Run the Test**
Run your refactored script back in Chromium to confirm it passes without opening the Inspector UI:

```bash
npx playwright test tests/first-test.spec.js --project=chromium
```

