is the job market huge for it ÿThe job market for SDETs is not as massive as general Software Engineering by sheer volume of open seats, but it is one of the most unsaturated, high-demand niches in tech. 

It is widely considered the smartest "backdoor" into the software industry for new grads. Here is exactly why the market works in your favor:

*   **Low Competition from Peers:** 95% of computer science students graduate and immediately apply for standard Frontend, Backend, or AI roles. Very few students intentionally build an SDET portfolio. When you apply for a Junior SDET role, you are competing in a much smaller, less crowded applicant pool.
*   **The Death of Manual Testing:** Companies are actively eliminating manual QA teams. They cannot afford to wait days for humans to click through websites before a release. They are replacing 5 manual testers with 1 or 2 SDETs who can automate those clicks in a CI/CD pipeline. 
*   **The AI Code Boom:** Because AI tools (like GitHub Copilot) are helping developers write feature code faster than ever, companies are shipping features at breakneck speed. This creates a massive bottleneck: they desperately need automated testing infrastructure to ensure this fast-paced code doesn't break production.
*   **The Golden Ratio:** Most modern engineering teams operate on a ratio of roughly 4 developers to 1 SDET. While there are fewer total jobs than standard developer roles, there are also drastically fewer qualified people applying for them.

The market for entry-level tech jobs is undeniably tough across the board right now. However, an applicant with a polished, CI/CD-integrated Playwright portfolio will land interviews for QA Automation roles significantly faster than a generic bootcamp grad applying for junior React developer roles.

